Ask a vendor what a WhatsApp chatbot costs and you'll usually get one number:
their monthly plan. Ask Meta and you'll get a rate card with four decimal
places. Neither is the figure you actually care about, which is what lands on
your company card every month once the bot is answering real customers.

That figure changed on **1 October 2026**, when Meta resumed charging for
service replies, which had been free since November 2024. So it's a good
moment to do the sums properly: Meta's fees, the provider's markup, the setup,
and the alternative of a flat monthly fee.

## First: do you need more than the free app?

The free WhatsApp Business app is genuinely good for a small team. You get a
business profile, a catalogue, quick replies and labels, and you can run it on
one phone plus up to four linked devices
([WhatsApp Help Center](https://faq.whatsapp.com/647349420360876)). Broadcasts
from the app only reach customers who have saved your number
([WhatsApp Help Center](https://faq.whatsapp.com/653415899610349)).

You've outgrown it when:

- **Enquiries arrive faster than people can answer**, especially in the
  evening, when
  [buyers message and nobody's at the desk](/blog/whatsapp-ai-agent-for-business).
- **Several staff share one number** and you're passing a phone around.
- **You want replies, lead capture and follow-ups to happen automatically**,
  written into a CRM rather than someone's memory.
- **You need to message customers who haven't saved your number**, such as
  order updates or opted-in promotions.

At that point there are two broad ways to put a chatbot on WhatsApp. You can
build on Meta's WhatsApp Business Platform (the "API"), usually through a
provider, and pay per message. Or you can use a product priced as a flat
monthly fee. Here's what each really costs.

## What Meta charges in Malaysia (from 1 October 2026)

Meta bills per **delivered** message, priced by message category and by the
recipient's country code. Messages your customers send you are free. These are
the Malaysia rates on Meta's MYR rate card, effective 1 October 2026
([Meta pricing docs](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/whatsapp/pricing)):

| Message category | What it's for | Rate per delivered message |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Service | Free-form replies within 24 hours of the customer's last message | RM 0.0564, first 1,000 a month free |
| Utility | Order, booking and payment updates (templates) | RM 0.0564 |
| Authentication | One-time passcodes (templates) | RM 0.0564 |
| Marketing | Promotions and broadcasts (templates) | RM 0.3467 |

Four details matter more than the headline rates:

1. **It's per message, not per conversation.** A bot that answers one enquiry
   in four separate bubbles has sent four service messages.
2. **The 1,000 free service messages are per phone number, per month**, and
   don't roll over. Meta charges from the 1,001st.
3. **Utility messages sent inside the 24-hour window are charged again.** They
   were free from July 2025 until 30 September 2026.
4. **Rates can move every quarter.** Meta's pricing calendar allows updates on
   1 January, 1 April, 1 July and 1 October.

There are ways to pay less. If a customer reaches you from a Click to WhatsApp
ad and you reply in time, Meta opens a free entry point window of up to seven
days in which marketing, utility, authentication and service messages aren't
charged. Meta also now sells its own AI, Meta Business Agent, at US$2.00 per
million tokens, which Meta estimates at roughly 4–5 US cents per message, with
no free tier.

## What the provider adds

Few SMEs connect to Meta directly, because it takes developers and hosting.
Most go through a Business Solution Provider or a chatbot platform that
charges on top of Meta's fees. Two common pricing shapes:

- **A per-message markup.** Twilio, for example, charges US$0.005 per message,
  inbound or outbound, and passes Meta's fees through on top
  ([Twilio WhatsApp pricing](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/whatsapp/pricing)).
- **A monthly subscription plus Meta's fees.** Wati, for example, lists plans
  at US$59 and US$119 a month billed annually, with message charges extra and
  its AI agents sold as a separately priced add-on
  ([Wati pricing](https://www.wati.io/pricing/)).

Then there's the chatbot itself. Unless the platform's AI is included, you're
paying for an AI model or a bot builder, plus someone to write the flows,
connect your CRM and keep the answers current. Some providers include
onboarding; others charge a one-off fee or an hourly rate.

None of this is a criticism of those providers. The API route is the right
choice for plenty of businesses, particularly for high-volume broadcasts and
order notifications. The point is that Meta's rate card is only one line on
the bill.

## A worked example: 2,000 enquiries a month

**This is an illustrative estimate, not a quote.** Real bills depend on how
chatty your bot is, your provider, your plan and the exchange rate. We've
converted US dollars at about RM 4.03, the ratio implied by Meta's own USD and
MYR rate cards for Malaysia.

The assumptions: a Malaysian SME receives 2,000 WhatsApp enquiries a month,
and the bot sends an average of four replies per enquiry, so 8,000 service
messages. The business also sends 300 order confirmations and two promotional
broadcasts to 500 opted-in customers each, so 1,000 marketing messages.

| Monthly line item | Per-message API route (subscription-style provider) | Flat fee (Corpi Growth) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Meta service messages: (8,000 − 1,000 free) × RM 0.0564 | RM 394.80 | RM 0 |
| Provider platform | ~RM 480 (US$119 plan, billed annually) | RM 500 (up to 2,000 conversations) |
| AI for the chatbot | Add-on, priced separately | Included up to tier limits |
| **Replies, per month** | **~RM 875 + AI** | **RM 500** |
| Meta utility messages: 300 × RM 0.0564 | RM 16.92 | Not in the published Corpi tiers |
| Meta marketing messages: 1,000 × RM 0.3467 | RM 346.70 | Not in the published Corpi tiers |
| One-off setup | Varies: some providers charge none, others charge for onboarding | From RM 1,500 |

Over a first year, replies alone come to roughly **RM 10,500 plus AI** on the
per-message route, against **RM 7,500** for the flat fee (RM 1,500 setup plus
12 × RM 500).

Two sensitivities worth noticing. If your bot answers in two messages instead
of four, Meta's service charge drops from RM 394.80 to RM 169.20. A tighter
bot is cheaper on a per-message plan. And on a per-message markup model like
Twilio's, the same 8,000 replies plus a similar number of customer messages
would add about US$80 (roughly RM 320) in provider fees, before any chatbot
software.

Marketing is where per-message pricing bites hardest: at RM 0.3467 each, one
broadcast to 1,000 customers costs about RM 347 in Meta fees alone. Budget it
as a campaign cost, not an afterthought.

## Where a flat fee fits

The other way to price this is the way we price
[Corpi](/corpi?utm_source=blog&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=corpi-blog-whatsapp-chatbot-cost-malaysia&utm_content=cta-inline-en-a),
our WhatsApp AI sales agent. It runs on your own WhatsApp Business number,
which you link by scanning a QR code, much like WhatsApp Web. It isn't billed
per message by Meta. You pay one setup fee and one monthly fee.

What it does:

- **Replies in English, BM, Mandarin or Manglish**, in whichever language the
  customer wrote in.
- **Qualifies through conversation**: need, budget and location, without a
  form.
- **Saves every lead into Notion**, with name, phone, objectives, budget and a
  conversation summary.
- **Hands off to a human** with the full thread when a customer asks for a
  person, and says so when it doesn't know an answer rather than inventing one.

The pricing, as published on the
[Corpi page](/corpi?utm_source=blog&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=corpi-blog-whatsapp-chatbot-cost-malaysia&utm_content=cta-inline-en-b):

| Tier | Monthly | Conversations |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Starter | RM 300 (US$64) | Up to 500 a month |
| Growth | RM 500 (US$110) | Up to 2,000 a month |
| Scale | RM 800 (US$170) | Unlimited |

Setup is from RM 1,500 (US$320), depending on scope. Monthly fees include
hosting, the dashboard, the Notion CRM and support. AI usage above tier limits
is billed at cost plus 20%, and there's a three-month minimum, then
month-to-month.

The honest trade-off: a flat fee buys predictability for the inbound side of
WhatsApp, the enquiries you have to answer quickly whether it's 3pm or 11pm.
It isn't a bulk broadcast or notification engine. If outbound templates at
volume are central to how you sell, budget for per-message pricing for that
part.

## Five questions to ask any WhatsApp chatbot vendor

Whoever you talk to, including us:

1. On average, how many messages does your bot send per enquiry?
2. Are Meta's fees passed through at cost, or marked up?
3. Is the AI included, and what happens when I exceed the limit?
4. What does setup include, and who updates the bot's answers later?
5. What's the minimum commitment, and how do I export my leads if I leave?

If a quote can't answer the first two, it isn't the full cost.

